@ title: The division lever — the +0.049 headroom
@ subtitle: a huge, proven gain that no hand-rule can reach
@ source: learning/ensemble_work/div_oracle.py

--- note
## Where the points actually are
The metric is `edge_J + 0.1·division_J`. For pilkwang, **division_J = 0** — it recovers **0 of 8**
GT divisions on the golden-12. That 0.1 term is completely untapped. How much is it worth? We built
an **oracle** (uses the GT to add the correct divisions) to measure the ceiling.

--- image
learning/assets/division_event.png
A division: one parent at t splits into two daughters at t+1 that both continue to t+2. The metric only counts it as a true positive if the parent matches within 7 µm AND both children continue — a strict topology.

--- note
### The oracle — recover just 4 divisions, gain +0.049
**[Data — measured]** Of the 8 GT divisions, **4 are recoverable** (both daughters are present as
detections). Adding exactly those 4 lifts the score from **0.870 → 0.919**:
--- output
| variant | golden-12 | division_J |
|---|---|---|
| base (pilkwang) | 0.870 | 0.000 (0/8) |
| **oracle: +4 real divisions** | **0.919** | 0.50 (4/8) |

--- note
### But no hand-rule can find them — we proved it three ways
**[Domain — the hard truth]** The real 2nd daughter is **8–14 µm** from the parent and is usually
**not** the nearest cell — at ~250 cells/frame it's buried among ~10 decoys. Every no-training
method got **0 true divisions**:
--- output
| method to pick the 2nd daughter | true divisions found |
|---|---|
| geometric (nearest, tight gates) | 0 |
| pilkwang's own edge-model probabilities | 0 |
| mitotic image signature (brightness+symmetry) | 0 |

--- note
### The conclusion
**[Domain]** The daughters are geometrically indistinguishable from random neighbours — the signal
that separates them is **learned**, not geometric. So the +0.049 is real but reachable only by
**training** the model to find divisions (`rs04`).
--- shape
ceiling: **+0.049**<br>hand-rules: **0 TP**<br>→ must be trained

